 "scenes": [
  {
   "id": "intro",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "How can we say a problem is hard, when nobody has proved that it has no fast algorithm? By comparison: if a fast algorithm for it would also solve a problem known to be hard. That comparison is a reduction, and it is the main tool of this module."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "head"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Reductions and NP-completeness"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Here are the seven stages."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "stages"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Reductions, what they tell us, NP-hard and NP-complete, and a worked reduction."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "stages[0..=3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Then the recipe for NP-completeness proofs, the family tree of NP-complete problems, and an exam question."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "stages[4..=6]"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "rd",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 1: a polynomial-time reduction from A to B is a function f that turns any instance of A into an instance of B."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 1 of 7: reductions"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "stages"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "rd"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "rd[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "f must run in polynomial time."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "rd[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "And it must preserve the answer: x is a yes-instance of A exactly when f of x is a yes-instance of B."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "rd[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Then any algorithm for B also decides A: transform the input, and ask B."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "rd[3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "An everyday example: is a number odd? Reduce it to: is its last digit odd? Ten easy cases answer every number."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "rd[4]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "A ≤p B: transform A's input into B's"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "wt",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 2: if A reduces to B, and B is in P, then A is in P too: the transformation plus B's algorithm stays polynomial."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 2 of 7: what it tells us"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "rd"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "wt"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "wt[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Turned round: if A is hard, B must be hard too, or A would be easy."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "wt[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "So read the symbol as: A is no harder than B."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "wt[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The direction is the commonest mistake. To prove a new problem B hard, reduce a known hard problem to B, not B to it."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "wt[3]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "reduce the known hard problem TO the new one"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "nc",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 3: a problem is NP-hard if every problem in NP reduces to it."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 3 of 7: NP-complete"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "wt"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "nc"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "nc[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "It is NP-complete if it is NP-hard and also in NP: the hardest problems inside NP."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "nc[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The key theorem: if any single NP-complete problem has a polynomial algorithm, then every problem in NP does, and P equals NP."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "nc[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Equivalently, if P is not NP, no NP-complete problem can be solved in polynomial time. That is why NP-complete means: stop looking for a fast exact algorithm."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "nc[3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Some problems are NP-hard without being in NP, such as the optimisation versions, which do not have yes-or-no answers."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "nc[4]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "NPC = in NP + NP-hard"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Thousands of practical problems are now known to be NP-complete: timetabling, routing, packing, circuit layout, protein folding models. They stand or fall together."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "nc[2]"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ex",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 4: Cormen's reduction from Hamiltonian cycle to the travelling salesman. Start with any graph G: here, four vertices and five edges."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 4 of 7: HAM-CYCLE ≤p TSP"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "nc"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "g"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "g.a"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Build a complete graph on the same vertices. An edge costs 0 if it is in G, and 1 if it is not. Here only b to d is missing from G, so it costs 1."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "k"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "k.b->d",
       "delay": 0.5
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Ask TSP: is there a tour of cost at most k equal to 0?"
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "k.a"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "A tour of cost 0 uses only edges of G, and visits every vertex once: exactly a Hamiltonian cycle. And a Hamiltonian cycle in G is a tour of cost 0. So the answers agree."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "k.a->b"
      },
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "k.b->c",
       "delay": 0.2
      },
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "k.c->d",
       "delay": 0.4
      },
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "k.a->d",
       "delay": 0.6
      },
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "g.a->b"
      },
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "g.b->c",
       "delay": 0.2
      },
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "g.c->d",
       "delay": 0.4
      },
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "g.d->a",
       "delay": 0.6
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Building the complete graph takes O of n squared time: polynomial. So Hamiltonian cycle reduces to TSP, and since Hamiltonian cycle is NP-complete, TSP is NP-hard."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "HAM-CYCLE ≤p TSP: cost 0 for edges of G"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Notice what the reduction does not do: it never searches for a cycle. It only rewrites the question. All the hard work is left to whoever solves TSP."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "k.b->d"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "how",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 5: the recipe for proving a problem X NP-complete. First, show X is in NP: give a certificate and a fast verifier."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 5 of 7: the recipe"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "g"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "k"
      },
