      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "how[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Second, choose a problem Y already known to be NP-complete."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "how[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Third, design a transformation from instances of Y to instances of X."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "how[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Fourth, prove both directions: a yes for Y gives a yes for X, and a yes for X gives a yes for Y."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "how[3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Fifth, check the transformation is polynomial. Then X is NP-hard, and with step 1, NP-complete."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "how[4]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "in NP + known NPC ≤p X"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "tree",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 6: it all starts with circuit satisfiability. Cook and Levin showed directly that every NP problem reduces to it. It is the first NP-complete problem."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 6 of 7: the family tree"
